## 2. SACRED FINANCIAL MODELING

### 2.1 The Philosophy

Thomas Laffont considers financial models to be a **"sacred place"** — not a spreadsheet, but a mathematical story.

**Core principles:**
- Build models **from scratch, cell by cell** — never edit thousand-line sell-side templates
- **Ruthlessly eliminate** any line items that do not drive the core investment thesis
- The model must serve as a **direct mathematical reflection of the story** from the top line to the bottom line
- Apply **"gift wrapping" meticulousness** — every cell, line, font, and background color has a specific narrative purpose designed to capture executive attention

### 2.2 What NOT to Do

| Common Practice | Laffont's Rejection |
|----------------|-------------------|
| Thousand-line sell-side templates | "I'm not saying editing is wrong, but we build from scratch" |
| Standard reporting lines that don't drive thesis | Ruthlessly removed |
| Generic formatting | Every cell styled with narrative intent |
| Automated data pulls | Manual input forces understanding of mechanics |

### 2.3 The Apple Pricing Lesson

**The mistake:** Thomas projected iPhone ARPU to decline 5-10% annually — standard for consumer electronics — when in reality the price *doubled*.

**The lesson:** Never blindly accept industry-standard inputs. The model must strictly reflect YOUR specific thesis, not conventional wisdom. If you'd built the model from scratch thinking about Apple's unique pricing power, you wouldn't have defaulted to "prices decline."

**What they got right:** Unit sales projections were mostly accurate.

**What they missed:** The magnitude of services revenue and ecosystem lock-in as additional TAMs.

