## 12. THE SPIELBERG THREE-SENTENCE RULE

### 12.1 Origin Story

When Thomas Laffont was a trainee in the CAA mailroom (Creative Artists Agency), all trainees were brought into a meeting with director **Steven Spielberg**.

**Spielberg told them:** "Every great story can be pitched in three sentences no matter what the story was."

Thomas challenged him to pitch a complex movie. **Spielberg easily distilled the essence into three sentences.**

**The lesson:** "It takes a true understanding of story to be able to crystallize it in three sentences."

### 12.2 Application to Investing

**The rule:** If you truly understand a company's story and the pivot points that will make or break the stock, you MUST be able to pitch it in three sentences.

- If a thesis requires a rambling, multi-page explanation → the investor likely doesn't understand the core drivers
- The three-sentence format forces clarity on the *pivot points* — the 2-3 things that determine success or failure

### 12.3 Relation to the Financial Model

The three-sentence thesis and the "sacred" financial model are two sides of the same coin:
- **The model:** The mathematical story from top line to bottom line
- **The three sentences:** The verbal distillation of that mathematical story

Both must be ruthlessly stripped of anything that doesn't drive the core narrative.

